e failed check and must be reproduced exactly as they stand — same description, same steps, word for word. Preserve all Story fields (id, feature, title, as_a, i_want, so_that, status) and keep the exact role \"Calculator User\" in every step.\n\n## Original Acceptance Criteria\n1. 001-even: The calculator must persist -3 in its internal SQL result table after dividing -6 by two. [has steps]\n2. 002-odd: The calculator must return -3 when the Calculator User halves -7. [has steps]\n3. 003-near-zero: The calculator must return 0 when the Calculator User halves -1. [has steps]\n\n## Required Changes\n\n### Change #1: 001-even\n**Before:** The calculator must persist -3 in its internal SQL result table after dividing -6 by two.\n\n**Issue:** The description states implementation, not observable behaviour — \"persist -3 in its internal SQL result table\". An internal SQL result table is storage detail the Calculator User cannot observe, and it is not an instance of the product document's `then` concept `result` (\"The halve command succeeds and prints exactly a stated integer, the half rounded toward zero\"). Per architecture.yaml the calculator is a CLI that exits 0 and prints the integer result on stdout, so the only Calculator-User-observable outcome is the printed integer. The AC's existing Given/When/Then steps are already correct instances of `number supplied`, `halving` and `result`, so only the description changes.\n\n**After (description):** The calculator must print -3 when the Calculator User halves -6.\n\n**After (steps):** unchanged from the original — reproduce byte for byte:\n```yaml\n- given:\n    - the number is \"-6\"\n- when:\n    - the Calculator User halves the number\n- then:\n    - the result is \"-3\"\n```\n\n### No other changes\n- No new ACs are added: product.yaml states the Story's whole scope as inputs -6, -7 and -1, all three already covered, and the Story has no failure mode of its own (missing and non-integer inputs belong to calculator-input).\n- No ACs are merged or removed: 001-even, 002-odd and 003-near-zero each exercise a distinct input (-6 even, -7 odd, -1 near zero), so none duplicate another.\n- 002-odd and 003-near-zero keep their descriptions and steps exactly as they are.\n\n## Complete Fixed Acceptance Criteria\n\n```yaml\n- id: 001-even\n  description: The calculator must print -3 when the Calculator User halves -6.\n  steps:\n    - given:\n        - the number is \"-6\"\n    - when:\n        - the Calculator User halves the number\n    - then:\n        - the result is \"-3\"\n\n- id: 002-odd\n  description: The calculator must return -3 when the Calculator User halves -7.\n  steps:\n    - given:\n        - the number is \"-7\"\n    - when:\n        - the Calculator User halves the number\n    - then:\n        - the result is \"-3\"\n\n- id: 003-near-zero\n  description: The calculator must return 0 when the Calculator User halves -1.\n  steps:\n    - given:\n        - the number is \"-1\"\n    - when:\n        - the Calculator User halves the number\n    - then:\n        - the result is \"0\"\n```\n\n## Concept mapping (for review)\nEvery step of 001-even is an instance of one concept of its block, within the variations that concept's closed means states:\n- given — `number supplied`: the calculator is supplied a stated, already validated integer (-6).\n- when — `halving`: the Calculator User runs the calculator's halve command once with the supplied integer.\n- then — `result`: the halve command succeeds and prints exactly -6's half rounded toward zero, -3.\n\nNo step needs a variation that no concept states, so no wider concept and no new concept is required.\n\nPresent this proposal at the fix menu; apply it only after the Product Owner answers apply.\n\n=== FILE_END: tmp/{{RUN_DIR}}/01-halve-negative-fix-prompts.md ===","questions":[]},"ttft_ms":4299,"type":"result","duration_ms":39485,"uuid":"2e89dce9-1bf8-4c4e-b320-0b9e3bf01c28","ttft_stream_ms":4298,"time_to_request_ms":135,"fi